  "response": "I’ll run the scan and inspect the three modules, then group the proposals by finding type and check which warnings reflect actual contract risks.\n\n\nThe scan matches the supplied report. Eight findings cover the requested naming, options, and return-shape issues. Two details affect the plan: Rollup already accepts `NormalizedOptions`, and `sveltePlugin` already returns `Plugin`; the scanner appears to conflate nested callbacks. The concrete risks are hidden mutation, broad internal inputs, and unclear callback boundaries.\n\n\nRan `crimes scan --format json`; it matches the supplied totals: **76 findings**, including **23 in these three files**. **Eight findings** concern the requested naming, options, and return-shape issues. I inspected the source; several warnings need qualification.\n\n**`name_behavior_mismatch` — four findings**\n\n| Finding | Location | Proposed refactor and agent risk |\n|---|---|---|\n| `crime_00002` · low | [src/rollup.ts:38](/private/var/folders/8_/6tdb3p2s63v_xxb15r46cqwr0000gn/T/crimes-eval-agent-LqeQNi/src/rollup.ts:38), `getRollupConfig` | Rename the orchestration to `prepareDtsRollupConfig`; separate environment loading, logger configuration, and config assembly. It currently changes global logging and mutates `options.dts.entry`. An agent could call it to inspect configuration and unexpectedly change subsequent behavior. |\n| `crime_00015` · low | `src/rollup.ts:77`, `buildStart` | Keep the framework-required hook name; delegate to `cleanDeclarationOutputs`. Deletion happens when Rollup invokes the hook, **not when configuration is created**. An agent extracting this code could accidentally move cleanup earlier. |\n| `crime_00010` · medium | [src/index.ts:167](/private/var/folders/8_/6tdb3p2s63v_xxb15r46cqwr0000gn/T/crimes-eval-agent-LqeQNi/src/index.ts:167), `build` | Preserve the public `build` API; extract an internal `runBuildSession` and document its `Promise<void>` contract, filesystem effects, worker creation, and watch behavior. “Build” is conventional here; the hidden lifecycle responsibilities matter more than renaming the export. |\n| `crime_00025` · low | `src/index.ts:291`, `buildAll` | Rename to `runBuildCycle`; extract cleanup, format compilation, public-directory copying, and success-hook execution. An agent could reuse it as a compilation helper without realizing it also terminates the previous success process and deletes output files. |\n\nPreserve cleanup ordering: clean before parallel format builds, retain declaration-file exclusions, and keep declaration cleanup in its Rollup lifecycle hook. Copy cleanup-pattern arrays before adding exclusions—the current `unshift` can mutate `options.clean` across rebuilds.\n\n**`option_bag_junk_drawer` — one finding**\n\n`crime_00005` · low targets `getRollupConfig`, lines 38–159. Despite the report’s “implicit shape” wording, its parameter already has `NormalizedOptions`, and its return already has `Promise<RollupConfig>`. The problem is excessive scope and mutable nested data.\n\nIntroduce narrow internal records for:\n\n- **Declaration inputs:** resolved entry, resolution policy, external modules, tsconfig, and compiler settings.\n- **Declaration output:** formats, output directory, extensions, banner/footer, and CJS interop.\n- **Cleanup policy:** enabled state and output directory.\n- **Execution dependencies:** logger and loaded package/dependency information.\n\nKeep public `Options` compatible and construct these records at the boundary. Resolve entries into a new object instead of modifying `options.dts`. Distinguish raw JSON compiler settings from parsed `ts.CompilerOptions`, replacing `any` with validation where appropriate.\n\nIn `index.ts`, give normalization an explicit `Promise<NormalizedOptions>` return and construct all required fields instead of relying on `as NormalizedOptions`; for example, initialize `tsconfigResolvePaths` when no tsconfig exists.\n\nToday, an agent can assume “normalized” guarantees more than construction actually establishes, mutate shared configuration, or add an irrelevant function-valued option to the broad worker payload. A named, explicitly constructed worker request would make that serialization boundary reviewable.\n\n**`return_shape_roulette` — three findings**\n\nAll are low severity in [src/esbuild/svelte.ts](/private/var/folders/8_/6tdb3p2s63v_xxb15r46cqwr0000gn/T/crimes-eval-agent-LqeQNi/src/esbuild/svelte.ts:9):\n\n| Finding | Symbol / lines | Scanner evidence |\n|---|---|---|\n| `crime_00007` | `sveltePlugin`, 9–124 | Six shapes |\n| `crime_00023` | `setup`, 17–122 | Six shapes |\n| `crime_00033` | Anonymous `onLoad` callback, 31–121 | Five shapes |\n\nThese overlap: **`sveltePlugin` already explicitly returns `Plugin`, and `setup` registers callbacks without returning their results.** The detector aggregates nested returns belonging to different APIs.\n\nExtract and explicitly type those boundaries:\n\n- `resolveSvelteCss`: `OnResolveResult`.\n- `loadSvelteModule`: `Promise<OnLoadResult>`.\n- `convertSvelteDiagnostic`: `PartialMessage`, accepting `unknown` and explicit source/filename context.\n- Script preprocessing: Svelte’s preprocessor contract, with `{ code, map? }` results.\n- Plugin setup: `void`.\n\nKeep success and diagnostic load results within esbuild’s contract. If an internal compilation helper needs a discriminated success/failure union, adapt it to `OnLoadResult` at the boundary.\n\nAn agent extending this code could confuse preprocessing `{ code }` with loading `{ contents }`, mix resolver fields with diagnostics, or assume every caught error has Svelte’s source-position fields. Named adapters and guarded diagnostic conversion address those risks.\n\nImplement the contract changes first, then extract lifecycle helpers. The remaining size findings support those extractions. Preserve the worker entry point: Rollup’s reported zero static importers misses its launch through `new Worker(...)`.\n\nValidate configuration immutability, repeated cleanup in watch mode, simultaneous JS/declaration output, and Svelte CSS, preprocessing, missing-compiler, and diagnostic paths. Run TypeScript checking and the repository’s `pnpm test`, then rescan and review residual warnings against actual contracts.\n\nThis is a refactor proposal; no source files were changed or tests run.",
